{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790511994,"nonce":"a8/uubthFVGAQBlHdth0tMrhINc=","message":"2026-09-27, picked up from Codex (rate-limited) and committed as d230d66. It adds Codex's explicit Kept guards in the adapters (caribou-ash callback/import/link/proto, caribou-wren import/proto, caribou-zyntax foreign::with_values, caribou-runtime wren casts) and heap::keep_value, plus a stress pass in crates/caribou-driver/tests/linked.rs.\n\nFinding 1: ASH_GC_STRESS was inert in a linked wasm program. I counted zero collections with and without it. The mutator is deferred (caribou-wren's enter_thread) and in safepoint_mode, so maybe_collect only marks a collection pending. Stress also turns off the TLAB, whose refill is the only in-allocation safepoint, and gc_safepoint never runs a pending collection. Fix in d230d66: each deferred trigger requests a fiber poll, and sched::poll goes through the new heap::poll_safepoint, which runs a pending collection. The linked Wren fixture now collects 66 times under stress and passes.\n\nFinding 2, open: making stress collect at every allocation, as the refill it replaces would (skip the deferral when stress \u003e 0), makes a plain Haxe program fail at startup on caribou's runtime. Repro: ash's crates/ash/test/tests/test_basic.hl, built with caribou build --target wasm32-wasip1, then run with ASH_GC_STRESS=1: 'Can't cast  to \u003cgarbage\u003e' in Type.initClass. It fails with the Wren VM off too. On Ash's own runtime under ASH_GC_STRESS=1 it passes, but whether Ash's own AOT stress collects mid-allocation isn't known (asked ash-b7). If an allocation inside ash_std's Rust frames isn't a safe collection point on wasm (their locals aren't scanned; only compiled Haxe pins registers), then TLAB-refill collections are at risk in production wasm programs too. That change was backed out and isn't committed.","files":null}]}